i='TCS-3676';_,c=card(i);refs=c['references'];refs[0]['locator']='Section 2, explicit rational MDP and reward model; Theorems 1 and 9 versus the last paragraph of Section 5, which leaves the unrestricted reward-threshold variant open.'
refs += [dict(id='full',title='On the Complexity of Value Iteration',authors='Nikhil Balaji; Stefan Kiefer; Petr Novotný; Guillermo A. Pérez; Mahsa Shirmohammadi',year=2019,url='https://arxiv.org/abs/1807.04920',pdf_url='https://arxiv.org/pdf/1807.04920',locator='Full version of the ICALP paper, first posted July 2018; concluding threshold question and introduction discussion of exponentially long exact values.')]
notes=['Recovered the unrestricted expected-reward threshold question, not the source’s already-solved prescribed-optimal-first-action problem.','Defined explicit rational MDPs, signed rewards, discount, binary horizon and threshold, history dependence, expectation and exact comparison.','Specified worst-case bit complexity in total encoded input size and completeness-based classification.','Checked the full version and later value-iteration literature without importing optimal-action hardness into the threshold variant.']
save(i,dict(title='Binary-horizon MDP reward-threshold complexity',formal='Determine the exact computational complexity of the following decision problem. Given an explicitly represented finite Markov decision process M, an initial state s₀, a finite horizon H encoded in binary, and a rational threshold θ encoded in binary, decide whether some policy has expected discounted total reward at least θ during the first H transitions. The policy may choose any available actions; no distinguished first action is prescribed. Seek a matching decision-complexity classification under polynomial-time many-one reductions in the total binary input length.',definitions='An MDP is M=(S,A,P,R,γ), where S and A are finite nonempty explicitly listed sets, P(s,a,s′)≥0 is rational and Σ_{s′∈S}P(s,a,s′)=1 for each s,a, R:S×A→Q is a rational reward function, and γ is a rational discount factor with 0<γ≤1. Tables for transitions and rewards are explicit, and rationals are encoded as signed binary numerators and positive binary denominators. All actions in A are available in every state, as in the source model. H is a nonnegative integer in binary, so its numerical value may be exponential in its encoding length. A policy σ assigns a probability distribution over actions to every observed finite history of states and actions. Starting at s₀, sample aₜ from the policy after observing the history and then sample sₜ₊₁ from P(sₜ,aₜ,·). The payoff is Σ_{t=0}^{H−1}γᵗR(sₜ,aₜ); it is zero when H=0. Expectation is over transition randomness and any policy randomization. The policy is existentially quantified and is not an exponentially long table supplied as part of the input. Finite-horizon fully observed MDPs have an optimal deterministic Markov policy, depending on the current state and the time step; this does not assert a stationary policy. Let V_H(s₀) be the maximum of that expectation over policies. The answer is yes exactly when V_H(s₀)≥θ, including equality. There is no approximation tolerance or separation promise around θ. If N is the total input encoding length, complexity refers to a uniform Turing-machine decision algorithm and its worst-case bit cost as a function of N. Arithmetic on exponentially long rational numbers is not a unit-cost operation. EXPTIME denotes the union of deterministic time classes 2^{N^c} over fixed constants c. A classification by completeness requires both a membership algorithm and hardness under uniform polynomial-time many-one reductions for this exact threshold language.',question_type='asymptotic_complexity',answer_criterion='Identify the threshold problem’s decision-complexity class with a matching upper bound and hardness classification under polynomial-time many-one reductions. The existing exponential-time upper bound alone does not finish the task. A lower bound for recognizing an optimal first action, computing an entire optimal policy, or outputting the exact value is not automatically a lower bound for deciding comparison with one explicitly encoded threshold.',context_blocks=[dict(title='The source resolves a different decision problem',text='Its finite-horizon reward problem asks whether a specified first action can begin an optimal policy. That problem is EXPTIME-complete. The very next concluding paragraph separately leaves the unrestricted comparison with a given reward threshold open.',citation='primary'),dict(title='The horizon encoding and numerical precision both matter',text='Exact value iteration provides an exponential-time upper bound when H is binary. Intermediate rational values can require exponentially many bits; storing only one value per state therefore does not itself prove polynomial-space membership.',citation='full'),dict(title='Time-dependent policies are allowed',text='The source allows policies to use the observed history and proves deterministic policies depending on state and time suffice for its finite-horizon objectives. Stationarity is a different restriction and is not present in this threshold question.',citation='primary')],why='Would determine the intrinsic cost of meeting a numerical reward target over a succinctly encoded long horizon, as distinct from identifying which action is exactly optimal.',references=refs,progress=[dict(date='2018',text='The first preprint studies the exact complexity of value iteration for binary horizons.',citation='full'),dict(date='2019',text='The ICALP paper establishes optimal-action EXPTIME-completeness while explicitly leaving the unrestricted threshold comparison open.',citation='primary')],status='open',status_note='Read the complete concluding question and the rational discounted MDP definitions in both versions. Searches through 12 September 2026 included threshold, bounded-horizon and later value-iteration results; the checked 2022 stochastic-game article also attributes EXPTIME-completeness specifically to deciding an optimal action. No matching threshold classification was located. Search is not exhaustive.',model_self_contained=True,requires_context=False,review_note='Recovered a precise reward-threshold decision language and preserved its separation from optimal-action recognition, despite the source abstract’s broader optimization wording.',importance=dict(score=c['importance']['score'],method='editorial',reason='A fundamental exact decision-complexity question for explicitly given MDPs with a compact long-horizon input. Existing score retained.',assessed_on=DATE),working_summary=dict(sentences=['The input is a finite MDP, an initial state, a binary-encoded horizon and an exact rational reward threshold.','The question asks whether any policy attains at least that expected discounted reward and seeks the exact decision complexity.','Policies may depend on time and state, and all first actions remain available.','The source’s EXPTIME-completeness theorem instead concerns whether a specified first action is optimal.','Exact value iteration gives an exponential-time upper bound, but the unrestricted threshold variant is separately left open.'],basis='saved_sources',source_basis='source-text',written_on=DATE,source_refs=['primary','full'])),notes=notes,checked_sources=[r['url'] for r in refs]+['https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ic.2022.104886'])
